 To address this challenge,
 we propose an iterative method for approximating OT couplings,
 building on the Sinkhorn algorithm's concept. 
 Our approach introduces a novel, iterative scheme called "partial Sinkhorn" 
 and demonstrates its theoretical consistency.
 We also prove that the proposed method can be used to compute OT couplings
 that are both discrete and regularized,
 and we explore its connection to the OT equation. 
 Furthermore, we conduct an in-depth analysis of the method's convergence properties, 
 including the derivation of iterative formulas for OT couplings in both discrete 
 and regularized forms, 
 as well as the estimation of the coupling error and the OT distance. 
 To evaluate our results, we design a benchmark experiment 
 and report promising findings. 
 We believe that our partial Sinkhorn algorithm offers an efficient solution 
 for OT computation, 
 and we explore its applicability in real-world scenarios 
 by providing an example of using OT for generative models. 
 We anticipate that our proposed method will be useful in various machine learning 
 applications, 
 particularly those involving OT in data representation, 
 especially when working with high-dimensional data. 
